Attack Logic:
Musl's crypt() implementation enforces a 256-byte ceiling on password input (a KEY_MAX inherited from its SHA-512 crypt, present since ~2012). When a password exceeds this limit, musl doesn't return NULL (as glibc and POSIX convention expect) — it returns the string "*". This single-character asterisk is musl's generic "operation failed" sentinel.

In /etc/shadow, the password field * conventionally means "account locked — no valid password." Alpine Linux has shipped root's shadow entry as root:*:... since approximately Alpine 3.16. The intent is that no input should ever hash to *, so comparison always fails and login is denied.

When an attacker sends an oversized password (257–1024 bytes) to sshd: a diagram is provided below

Attacker                              sshd (Alpine/musl)
   |                                       |
   |-- SSH connect, password auth -------->|
   |   (password = 300+ bytes of 'A')      |
   |                                       |-- reads /etc/shadow
   |                                       |   stored_hash = "*" (locked root)
   |                                       |
   |                                       |-- calls crypt(oversized_pw, "*")
   |                                       |   musl KEY_MAX exceeded → returns "*"
   |                                       |
   |                                       |-- strcmp("*", "*") == 0
   |                                       |   MATCH → authentication succeeds
   |                                       |
   |<---------- shell as root -------------|

And then exfil can occur. 
